38,2,,status,Minato,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
39,2,1,talk,Kenji,,"Wow, I found some important stuff! The earth-moving firms are worried about clay subsoil and slippage risks. Also, we can't hoard the center or main gate without closing them. This might make the schedule tricky!","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"It seems the ground is as stubborn as the schedule is optimistic. My findings mirror this; the land is struggling with drainage and heat, while pedestrians are carving their own paths to the tram.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,1,talk,Minato,,"I found out that there are only seven people on the groundskeeping roll now, and it used to be eighteen. But there are some kind volunteer groups who help out on the weekends!","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,1,talk,Minako,,"My findings are concerning. While some structural work has held up, other surfaces have failed repeatedly. Furthermore, the cost of maintaining planted areas is skyrocketing compared to hard surfaces. We must prioritize durability over aesthetics.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,1,talk,Jonathan,,"@Minako, it seems we have found a common concern. The upkeep costs for planted areas are indeed rising sharply, and the habitat fund only covers those specific elements, leaving other costs to the city.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Kenji,,"@Jonathan @Minako, if plants are so expensive and the ground is slippery clay, maybe we should be careful about too much greenery! We need a plan that's easy to build and won't slide away.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Jonathan"",""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Kenji, a prudent observation. If we ignore the clay and the heat, we are merely designing a very expensive swamp. We need a plan that respects the pedestrians' desire for efficiency and the land's struggle to breathe.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,2,talk,Minato,,"I'm worried that if there are only seven people left to work, they might get too tired. Do you think the volunteers can help with the new things in the park too?","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,2,talk,Minako,,"@Minato, volunteers are a wonderful asset, but we cannot rely on them for core maintenance. Given the staffing shortage and rising costs, we need a design that is fundamentally low-maintenance and physically resilient.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Minato""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Minako, I completely agree. If the city is already struggling with staffing and rising costs, we cannot afford a plan that looks beautiful on paper but becomes a financial drain in three years. We need sustainable simplicity.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Kenji,,"I agree! Let's look for a plan that doesn't have too many complicated parts. If the ground is tricky and we don't have many workers, a simple and sturdy plan is the best way to go!","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"Precisely. We must avoid the temptation of a ""visionary"" design that ignores the reality of the tram-commuters' shortcuts and the drainage failures. A plan that fights the terrain is a plan destined for failure.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,3,talk,Minato,,"I hope the plan is simple so the seven workers don't have to work too hard. If it's easy to look after, maybe the volunteers will feel happy to help more too.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,3,talk,Minako,,Exactly. We must be wary of plans that promise a lush paradise but ignore the reality of our budget and labor. I will be looking for a design that prioritizes long-term structural integrity over temporary visual appeal.,"{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,3,talk,Jonathan,,It seems we are in agreement. We must ensure the chosen plan does not create a permanent deficit. A design that balances the habitat fund's requirements with low long-term city expenditure is the only responsible path.,"{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Kenji,,I'm glad we all agree! I'll be looking for the plan that's the most realistic to build and maintain. Let's see which one fits our needs best in the next round!,"{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
55,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,"Indeed. Let us hope the candidates offer a solution that acknowledges the tram-commuters' footprints and the soil's temperament, rather than a fantasy that collapses under the first summer heatwave. I shall see you all in the final round.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
56,2,4,talk,Minato,,I hope the plans are kind to the people who work there. I'll be looking for one that doesn't ask too much of the small team. See you all soon!,"{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
57,2,4,talk,Minako,,"I agree. We have a clear set of criteria now: durability, low maintenance, and realistic construction. I look forward to seeing which candidate actually respects these constraints. See you in the final round.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
58,2,4,talk,Jonathan,,I share your anticipation. Let us hope for a proposal that values fiscal prudence as much as it does public beauty. I will see you all in the final round.,"{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
